# Personal Azure setup

Use a dedicated resource group in Sami's subscription. Do not reuse the resource
group, SQL server, databases, or Container Apps environment of another project.
This is the personal-account fallback in Sprint1.md. Resource creation costs money;
a budget alert reports spending but does not stop it.

## 1. Prepare tools and choose names

Run these commands in Bash. Use the cd-deploy worktree until its PR is merged:

```bash
cd /home/coolss/Uni_Projects/sdd/group_assignment/pantrypal-devops/.worktrees/cd-deploy
az login
az account list --query '[].{name:name,id:id,isDefault:isDefault}' -o table
export SUBSCRIPTION='1ffa64df-6785-4731-83b0-629fe825fda4 '
az account set --subscription "$SUBSCRIPTION"
az group list --query '[].{name:name,location:location}' -o table
export PREFIX='sdd-BCSAI-g3B-personal'
export RG="rg-$PREFIX"
export LOCATION='westeurope'
export SWA_LOCATION='westeurope'
export REPO='matheusfunabashi/pantrypal-devops'
```

The suggested prefix mirrors the assignment naming and avoids guessing your class
and group number. If known, prefer `sdd-<class>-g<number>-personal` instead. Choose
once, before provisioning. SQL server names must be globally unique; if the name
is taken, adjust PREFIX before the first setup run. Do not change PREFIX halfway
through a partially completed setup.

Confirm the selected subscription and existing projects before proceeding.
Check regional availability and quota if SQL or Container Apps provisioning is
rejected; do not silently substitute a different database tier.

```bash
az account show --query '{name:name,id:id,user:user.name}' -o table
az extension add --name containerapp --upgrade
az provider register --namespace Microsoft.App --wait
az provider register --namespace Microsoft.OperationalInsights --wait
az provider register --namespace Microsoft.Sql --wait
az provider register --namespace Microsoft.Web --wait
az group create --name "$RG" --location "$LOCATION" --tags project=pantrypal owner=Thecoolss
```

In Azure Cost Management, create a monthly budget filtered to this resource group,
with alerts at 50%, 80%, and 100% of an amount you are comfortable spending.
The setup uses two serverless SQL databases (0.5–1 vCore, 15-minute auto-pause,
2 GB, locally redundant backups), two apps (0–1 replicas), and a Free Static Web
App. SQL storage and logs can still cost money while application compute is idle.

## 2. Provision using your owner account

Generate and save three different SQL passwords in your password manager.
Enter them interactively, so they do not enter shell history:

```bash
export SQL_ADMIN_USER='pantrypaladmin'
read -rsp 'SQL admin password: ' SQL_ADMIN_PASSWORD; echo
export SQL_ADMIN_PASSWORD
./infra/setup.sh
export SQL_SERVER="$PREFIX-sql"
export PANTRY_DB_NAME="$PREFIX-pantry-db"
export CATALOG_DB_NAME="$PREFIX-catalog-db"
read -rsp 'Pantry application password: ' PANTRY_DB_APP_PASSWORD; echo
export PANTRY_DB_APP_PASSWORD
read -rsp 'Catalog application password: ' CATALOG_DB_APP_PASSWORD; echo
export CATALOG_DB_APP_PASSWORD
./infra/create-db-users.sh
```

Docker must be running for the DB-user script. It temporarily allows your public
IP, connects over encrypted SQL with certificate validation, and removes the rule
on exit. If your machine uses a VPN, ensure Docker reaches Azure through the same
public IP. The application users each own only their own database.

Setup initially creates Container Apps using Microsoft's public quickstart image
on port 80. They do not run PantryPal or expose its /health yet. CD replaces these
images and sets port 8000 after database users and secrets exist. This avoids
starting application migrations without DB configuration.

Provisioning is resumable: existing resources are skipped. It does not reconcile
configuration drift. Inspect existing settings if rerunning after changes.

## 3. Give GitHub a resource-group-scoped identity

Keep your personal owner login for initial setup. The deployment identity needs
Contributor on this project resource group only. Run this with an account allowed
to create app registrations and role assignments:

```bash
set -o pipefail
az ad sp create-for-rbac \
  --name "$PREFIX-github" \
  --role Contributor \
  --scopes "/subscriptions/$SUBSCRIPTION/resourceGroups/$RG" \
  --sdk-auth |
  gh secret set AZURE_CREDENTIALS --repo "$REPO"
```

This sends the credential JSON directly to GitHub instead of writing sp.json.
Do not print it, commit it, or paste it into chat. Record the identity's app ID
and credential expiry in your password manager. If this fails after identity
creation, inspect that identity before retrying so you do not create duplicates.
Use an Azure CLI version supporting --sdk-auth; this is the credential format
expected by the current azure/login workflow.

## 4. Configure GitHub secrets and variables

Authenticate gh to an account with access to manage repository Actions settings.
The following API commands work with the older installed gh CLI, which lacks
the gh variable command.

```bash
printf '%s' "$PANTRY_DB_APP_PASSWORD" | gh secret set PANTRY_DB_APP_PASSWORD --repo "$REPO"
printf '%s' "$CATALOG_DB_APP_PASSWORD" | gh secret set CATALOG_DB_APP_PASSWORD --repo "$REPO"
az staticwebapp secrets list -g "$RG" -n "$PREFIX-ui" --query properties.apiKey -o tsv |
  gh secret set AZURE_STATIC_WEB_APPS_API_TOKEN --repo "$REPO"

export SQL_SERVER_FQDN
SQL_SERVER_FQDN=$(az sql server show -g "$RG" -n "$SQL_SERVER" --query fullyQualifiedDomainName -o tsv)
export PANTRY_URL
PANTRY_URL="https://$(az containerapp show -g "$RG" -n "$PREFIX-pantry" --query properties.configuration.ingress.fqdn -o tsv)"
export CATALOG_URL
CATALOG_URL="https://$(az containerapp show -g "$RG" -n "$PREFIX-catalog" --query properties.configuration.ingress.fqdn -o tsv)"
export UI_ORIGIN
UI_ORIGIN="https://$(az staticwebapp show -g "$RG" -n "$PREFIX-ui" --query defaultHostname -o tsv)"
read -rp 'Team contact email for Open Food Facts: ' TEAM_EMAIL
export OFF_USER_AGENT="PantryPal/0.1 ($TEAM_EMAIL)"

set_variable() {
  local name="$1" value="$2"
  if gh api "repos/$REPO/actions/variables/$name" --silent 2>/dev/null; then
    gh api --method PATCH "repos/$REPO/actions/variables/$name" -f name="$name" -f value="$value" --silent
  else
    gh api --method POST "repos/$REPO/actions/variables" -f name="$name" -f value="$value" --silent
  fi
}
set_variable AZURE_RG "$RG"
set_variable PREFIX "$PREFIX"
set_variable SQL_SERVER_FQDN "$SQL_SERVER_FQDN"
set_variable PANTRY_URL "$PANTRY_URL"
set_variable CATALOG_URL "$CATALOG_URL"
set_variable UI_ORIGIN "$UI_ORIGIN"
set_variable OFF_USER_AGENT "$OFF_USER_AGENT"
set_variable ENABLE_FEATURE_SMOKE_TESTS false
unset SQL_ADMIN_PASSWORD SQLCMDPASSWORD PANTRY_DB_APP_PASSWORD CATALOG_DB_APP_PASSWORD
```

Keep UI_ORIGIN as https://hostname with no trailing slash.
Do not put the SQL admin password in GitHub. Once variable names exist, updates
can also be made through GitHub Settings → Secrets and variables → Actions.

## 5. Merge and verify deployment

Before merging the deployment PR, make the two existing GHCR packages public in
GitHub Package settings. Confirm the latest image build passed. Request Matheus's
review of the CD changes and Gustave's review of the documentation as appropriate.
After approval and green checks, merge the deployment PR and watch the CD run.

CD runs CI, publishes SHA-tagged images, configures DB secrets and environment
variables, deploys both APIs and the static UI, and polls both health endpoints
concurrently for at most five minutes. Feature smoke tests remain disabled until
/products/{barcode} and /pantry exist and are deployed. Then set
ENABLE_FEATURE_SMOKE_TESTS=true and verify a fresh CD run.

```bash
curl --fail --show-error --max-time 60 "$PANTRY_URL/health"
curl --fail --show-error --max-time 60 "$CATALOG_URL/health"
printf 'Open the UI: %s\n' "$UI_ORIGIN"
for service in pantry catalog; do
  az containerapp show -g "$RG" -n "$PREFIX-$service" \
    --query '{image:properties.template.containers[0].image,scale:properties.template.scale,ingress:properties.configuration.ingress}' -o json
  az sql db show -g "$RG" -s "$SQL_SERVER" -n "$PREFIX-$service-db" \
    --query '{name:name,sku:sku,minCapacity:minCapacity,autoPauseDelay:autoPauseDelay,maxSizeBytes:maxSizeBytes}' -o json
done
```

Check that the UI shows both services Healthy, CORS_ORIGINS matches UI_ORIGIN,
image tags match the deployed main SHA, replicas are 0–1, and SQL settings match
the plan. After the database PRs deploy, verify migration logs and tables.
Use the Container Apps environment's Log Analytics workspace to inspect the
actual console-log table/schema, run requests against both APIs, and save a query
showing method, path, status, and duration for both.

Update the README live URL, ADR-3, and docs/scrum/sprint-1.md with the personal
subscription fallback and evidence of the first successful deployment.

## Password rotation

Rerun create-db-users.sh with new application passwords, update the corresponding
GitHub secrets, and rerun CD from main to apply them. Updating GitHub alone does
not rotate the SQL users. Coordinate rotation because running revisions temporarily
retain old credentials. Revoke/rotate any credential actually exposed.
